          "desc": "Differentiating both logarithmic terms correctly"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln\\left(\\frac{x-4}{x^2+4}\\right)\\right) = \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln(x-4) - \\ln(x^2+4))$\n$= \\frac{1}{x-4} - \\frac{2x}{x^2+4}$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Logarithm laws allow splitting quotients: $\\ln\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right) = \\ln u - \\ln v$, making differentiation much simpler than using the quotient rule directly.",
