          "Rewrite $\\ln\\left(\\frac{x-4}{x^2+4}\\right)$ as $\\ln(x-4) - \\ln(x^2+4)$.",
          "Differentiate $\\ln(x-4)$ to get $\\frac{1}{x-4}$.",
          "Differentiate $\\ln(x^2+4)$ to get $\\frac{2x}{x^2+4}$.",
          "Combine the terms: $\\frac{1}{x-4} - \\frac{2x}{x^2+4}$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Trying to differentiate the fraction inside the log directly via the quotient rule, which leads to complicated algebra."
      }
    },
    {
      "subpart": "b",
      "stem": "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln\\left(x\\sqrt{x^2+4}\\right)\\right)$",
      "final_answer": "$\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{x}{x^2+4}$",
      "mark_breakdown": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
